  { "@type": "Question", "name": "言語治療評估報告中的「誤吸」和「穿透」有什麼分別？", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "穿透（Penetration）是指食物或液體進入喉部但未越過聲帶，屬於警示訊號但嚴重程度較低。誤吸（Aspiration）是指食物或液體越過聲帶，進入氣管和肺部，是吸入性肺炎的主要成因。沉默式誤吸（Silent Aspiration）指誤吸時沒有咳嗽反應，是最危險的情況，只有儀器評估（VFSS 或 FEES）才能確認。" } },
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "IDDSI 飲食等級是如何從評估結果決定的？", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "言語治療師根據評估所見決定最合適的 IDDSI 等級：稀薄液體誤吸需要增稠液體（Level 1–4）；顯著谷殘留可能需要調整食物至 Level 4–5；咽部延遲嚴重則避免混合質感食物並調整液體稠度。IDDSI 等級的選擇是平衡安全性、營養充足及生活質量的結果，不應自行更改。" } },
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "什麼是「代償策略」，照顧者如何在日常執行？", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "代償策略是透過改變姿勢或進食方式來減少誤吸風險的方法。常見例子包括：下巴內收法（Chin Tuck，頭略前低適用於咽部延遲）、頭部轉側（Head Rotation，頭轉向較弱側適用於單側咽部無力）、用力吞嚥（Effortful Swallow，適用於谷殘留）及雙重吞嚥（每次吞嚥後再做一次空吞嚥以清除殘留）。照顧者應了解報告中指定的策略並在每次進食時一貫執行。" } },
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "如何要求安排言語治療覆查評估？", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "以下情況可要求覆查：患者整體狀況明顯改善（如中風後三至六個月）；出現新症狀如反覆肺炎或體重急跌；患者持續拒絕現時飲食等級影響營養；或上次評估已逾六個月。可透過主診醫生或醫務社工提出轉介要求；如公立系統等候時間過長，可自行聯絡私家言語治療師。" } }
];
const faqSltReportsEn = [
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "What is the difference between penetration and aspiration in an SLT report?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Penetration means food or liquid enters the larynx but does not pass below the vocal cords — a warning sign but less severe. Aspiration means food or liquid passes below the vocal cords into the trachea and lungs, and is the primary cause of aspiration pneumonia. Silent aspiration — aspiration without a cough response — is the most dangerous, and can only be confirmed by instrumental assessment (VFSS or FEES)." } },
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "How is the IDDSI level determined from assessment findings?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The SLT selects the most appropriate IDDSI level based on assessment findings: aspiration of thin liquids requires thickened drinks (Level 1–4); significant vallecular residue may indicate food adjustment to Level 4–5; severe pharyngeal delay means avoiding mixed-texture foods and increasing liquid thickness. The level is a balance of safety, adequate nutrition and quality of life — do not change it without clinical guidance." } },
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "What are compensatory strategies and how should caregivers apply them daily?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Compensatory strategies reduce aspiration risk by changing posture or feeding approach. Common examples include: chin tuck (head slightly forward, for pharyngeal delay); head rotation (turning towards the weaker side, for unilateral pharyngeal weakness); effortful swallow (swallowing with deliberate force, for vallecular residue); and double swallow (a second dry swallow after each mouthful to clear residue). Caregivers should identify the strategies specified in the patient's report and apply them consistently at every meal." } },
  { "@type": "Question", "name": "How do I request a repeat swallowing assessment?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Repeat assessment is appropriate when: the patient's condition has improved significantly (e.g., three to six months post-stroke); new symptoms have emerged such as recurrent pneumonia or sudden weight loss; the patient persistently refuses the current diet level affecting nutrition; or the last assessment was more than six months ago. Make the request through the attending doctor or medical social worker. If public waiting times are long, contact a private speech therapist directly." } }
];
